# e2fsprogs -- mke2fs and e2fsck. The graphical installer creates the root
# filesystem with mkfs.ext4 and the installed system fsck's that root at every
# boot, and nothing else in the tree can do either. Without this package there
# is no way to install Duct to a disk at all.
#
# Not an LFS package here, so it is pinned in the recipe rather than in
# versions.env. kernel.org rather than SourceForge: it is the maintainer's own
# publication point, and SRC_SHA256 makes the host an implementation detail
# rather than a trust decision.
SRC_URL=https://www.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/people/tytso/e2fsprogs/v1.47.2/e2fsprogs-1.47.2.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=7a959221c1b1cc6e28b7d7a4e204a2ffd8ec6d8a2de4461c482b64c5f4463cca
SRC_DIR=e2fsprogs-1.47.2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE THREE DISABLES ARE NOT TIDINESS. THE PACKAGE CANNOT INSTALL WITHOUT THEM.
#
# util-linux is already in this tree and already owns /usr/sbin/fsck,
# /usr/sbin/blkid, /usr/bin/uuidgen, libblkid and libuuid. tape treats two
# packages claiming one path as a HARD INSTALL ERROR WITH NO OVERRIDE
# (daemon/utils/install.go, CheckConflicts) -- so an e2fsprogs built with its
# own copies does not merely duplicate them, IT FAILS TO INSTALL. The error
# names a file conflict and says nothing about configure flags, so the natural
# instinct on meeting it is to look anywhere but here.
#
# The same instinct applies to reading this list: three disables invite someone
# to wonder which one is superstition and remove it. None of them are. What
# each one prevents, read from the source rather than assumed:
#
#   --disable-fsck      configure.ac:718 decides the default from $host_os, and
#                       only gnu* -- which is HURD, not Linux -- declines to
#                       build it. linux-gnu falls through to *) and BUILDS THE
#                       FSCK WRAPPER BY DEFAULT. This is the one that would bite
#                       every build, on every machine, immediately.
#
#   --disable-libuuid   Without it, configure probes for a system libuuid and
#                       builds its PRIVATE copy when it does not find one --
#                       which also installs /usr/bin/uuidgen. That makes the
#                       conflict CONDITIONAL ON THE BUILD ENVIRONMENT: fine in
#                       an image where util-linux is present, broken in one
#                       where it is not, with nothing in the recipe to explain
#                       the difference. Passing it makes the requirement
#                       explicit and turns a silent fallback into a hard error.
#
#   --disable-libblkid  Same shape; the private libblkid brings /usr/sbin/blkid.
#
# The consequence, and the reason util-linux is in [dependencies] rather than
# [dependencies.build]: with the private copies refused, e2fsprogs links
# against util-linux's libuuid and libblkid, so they must be there at RUN time.
#
# --disable-uuidd is REDUNDANT and is here as a statement rather than a
# requirement: configure already prints "Disabling uuidd by default". It is
# kept so that a future upstream default cannot quietly add a daemon to a
# package that exists to make two filesystems, and it is labelled so nobody
# mistakes it for load-bearing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--enable-elf-shlibs \
	--disable-fsck --disable-libuuid --disable-libblkid --disable-uuidd \
	--disable-nls"
